  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot k+6\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-7)+(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-8k+12+14-10 \\\\  & =-8k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8k+16 & =0 \\\\ 8k & =16 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
